module lib/hf/opts
  Every choice a Hartree-Fock run makes, in one record, `ScfOptions`, and the
  plain array of numbers the registry members read those choices from.

  A caller starts from `scf_defaults()` and changes the fields it wants with a
  record update: `ScfOptions { guess: GS_CORE, ..scf_defaults() }`. A registry
  member is called through `apply`, which passes numbers, functions and
  arrays but not records, so the SCF driver copies the record into a column
  array once per run (`opt_array`), and the members index it by the `OPT_*`
  column names defined here.

scf_defaults  @636641b0
  why   One place holds the production choices, so a program that wants a
        change names only that change, and the lock ladder and the library's
        tests all start from the same run
        [test "the defaults are the ones the table lists"].

scf_checked  @125e6ffc
  why   The integral stages each have several members that must agree; this is
        the switch that makes a run compute every member and assert agreement,
        which is how the tests in scf/ run.

opt_array  @ad1e07b6
  why   Registry members take their options through `apply`, which cannot pass
        a record; the column array is the record flattened, built in one place
        so no caller writes column numbers by hand
        [test "an update changes the fields it names and the column array follows"].
  o     the options record to flatten
  na    the number of alpha (spin-up) electrons
  nb    the number of beta (spin-down) electrons
  enuc  the repulsion energy between the nuclei, in hartree, which the SCF adds
        to the electronic energy
  a     the column array, `OPT_LEN` numbers; column `OPT_X` holds option X

opt_new  @9eb8a1ad
  why   A member's own tests need an options array without a molecule; this is
        the defaults with no electrons and no nuclear repulsion.

